#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222385} :activity/id #uuid "73bb2bcb-f3bd-5f4b-89b0-f776fa486afd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050010 :topic/id #uuid "2602318d-f33f-5fd6-9721-6e9791bc0896" :topic/math-academy-id 6383} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222519 :tutorial/content "When a Fourier series represents a function $f$, it is often much easier to differentiate the series term-by-term than to recompute a brand-new Fourier series from scratch. \n\nHowever, term-by-term differentiation is only guaranteed to work when the underlying periodic function is sufficiently \"well-behaved\".\n\nThe theorem about **term-by-term differentiation of Fourier series** states the following:\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nis a $2L$-periodic function which is\n\n- piecewise smooth, and\n\n- continuous.\n\nThen, the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} -\\dfrac{n\\pi a_n}{L}\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+\\dfrac{n\\pi b_n}{L}\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nIn other words, if $f(x)$ is the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ defined on $x \\in [-L,L),$ then the Fourier series of $f'(x)$ can be obtained by differentiating term-by-term the Fourier series of $f(x)$ provided that\n\n- $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-L,L),$\n\n- $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-L,L),$ and\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -L^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to L^-} F(x).$\n\n**Note:** The constant term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}$ disappears after differentiating, since $\\dfrac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\!\\left(\\dfrac{a_0}{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nIn the next slides, we will apply this theorem to a specific example to see how it works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "056cc662-995f-5011-b03f-b2d73577ee2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14690 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4e7a2c71-3dca-5950-82b1-233b8ec5ddca" :step/math-academy-id 602012 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222386}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221201 :question/id #uuid "6e45e60d-7c68-5c49-8ca2-5381af39cd47" :question/math-academy-id "e-21846"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049995}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425680}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"} :step/id #uuid "a890b2d2-6001-5870-bb47-e291f5a18935" :step/math-academy-id 602013 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221200 :question/id #uuid "669126b0-2995-549c-9a80-888bccd3acc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21845"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2e37d37-007c-569b-a44c-cb6a9d075371" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425682}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "76b70d71-8248-55db-9fbf-149752dab109" :step/math-academy-id 602016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222527 :tutorial/content "Term-by-term differentiation is a huge time-saver: it lets us obtain a Fourier series for $f'(x)$ without recomputing Fourier coefficients from scratch. \n\nBut differentiation also tends to make Fourier series \"less convergent\", it \"spends\" one power of $n$. So, we need to be careful about what the differentiated series actually does.\n\nIn a $2L$-periodic Fourier series, differentiating \n\n$$\n\\cos\\!\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right) \\qquad \\text{or} \\qquad \\sin\\!\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nintroduces a factor of $\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}$. So, very roughly,\n\n$$\n\\big(\\text{new coefficient size}\\big) \\approx n \\cdot \\big(\\text{old coefficient size}\\big).\n$$\n\nThat means one level of coefficient decay is \"used up\" when we differentiate.\n\nFor example, consider the so-called *triangle wave* and *sawtooth wave*, the $2\\pi$-periodic extensions from $x \\in  [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ of the functions $y=|x|$ and $y=x,$ respectively. \n\n![](images/90/90341fcc0828ee1fbefa229a1dc8b0a55ebf5912cfaf934585c73dae5ab720d0.png)\n\nTheir Fourier series are the following:\n\n$$\n|x| \\sim \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}-\\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{\\cos\\!\\big((2n-1)x\\big)}{(2n-1)^2}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nx \\sim 2\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin(nx)\n$$\n\nThe convergence of their corresponding derivatives is summarized in the table below.\n\n| **Function Type** | **Continuity** | **Fourier Series Coefficient Decay** | **Derivative Series Convergence** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Triangle wave | Continuous | $O\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right)$ | Converges \n(to the average of slopes) |\n| Sawtooth wave | Discontinuous | $O\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)$ | Diverges |\n\nNotice that:\n\n- For the continuous and piecewise smooth *triangle wave*:\n\n- The function has enough \"smoothness\" (continuity) that its Fourier coefficients decay quickly at a rate of $\\sim \\dfrac{1}{n^2}.$\n\n- Differentiation \"costs\" one level of decay: \n\n$$\nn \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n^2} = \\dfrac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nSince the terms $\\dfrac{1}{n}$ still approach $0,$ the series can converge. At the \"corners,\" it settles on the average of the left- and right-hand slopes.\n\n- For the discontinuous but piecewise smooth *sawtooth wave*:\n\n- The function's jump discontinuities \"use up\" the smoothness budget. The coefficients decay only at a rate of $\\sim \\dfrac{1}{n}.$\n\n- Differentiation cancels out the decay entirely: \n\n$$\nn \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n} = 1\n$$\n\nSince the terms of the series do not approach $0,$ the series fails the basic divergence test and does not converge." :tutorial/id #uuid "8eb8cb65-4c1f-5100-8e0e-28fa1bcfaf41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14759 :tutorial/title "A Word on Convergence"} :step/id #uuid "409a0a22-bfc0-5edd-ba4e-495f349d4ac6" :step/math-academy-id 602019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222389}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222521 :tutorial/content "Just like differentiation, integration can also be applied directly to a Fourier series. This is often useful when we already know the Fourier series of $f(x)$ and want a series for an antiderivative of $f(x)$ without recomputing Fourier coefficients from scratch.\n\nThe theorem about **term-by-term integration of Fourier series** states the following:\n\nSuppose that\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)+b_n\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)\n$$\n\nis a $2L$-periodic function which is piecewise continuous. Then, an antiderivative $F(x)$, where $F'(x) = f(x)$, can be found by integrating the Fourier series of $f(x)$ term-by-term:\n\n$$\nF(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}x + C + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\bigg( \\dfrac{a_n L}{n\\pi}\\sin\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg)-\\dfrac{b_n L}{n\\pi}\\cos\\bigg(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\bigg) \\bigg)\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant of integration.\n\n**Watch out!** Note the following caveats:\n\n1\\. The expression for the antiderivative $F(x)$ is not a Fourier series in the usual sense because it contains the linear term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}x.$\n\n2\\. Even if $f(x)$ is periodic, its antiderivative $F(x)$ is not necessarily periodic. In particular, if $a_0 \\neq 0,$ then the term $\\dfrac{a_0}{2}x$ grows without bound as $x$ increases.\n\nIf we want a periodic antiderivative, we typically need the average value of $f$ over one period to be $0$ (equivalently, $a_0=0$), so that the linear term disappears.\n\n**Note:** Integration is generally \"safer\" than differentiation for Fourier series. Differentiation can slow convergence or even destroy it, whereas integration divides the $n$th coefficient by $n,$ which typically improves convergence.\n\nNext, let's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "87052b6b-4aae-58df-8271-019f0cb8fb55" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14708 :tutorial/title "Integrating Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "d66cfd7d-a10e-557e-b6f8-239debcedcac" :step/math-academy-id 602020 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221202 :question/id #uuid "77c71e9d-c2a4-5a2c-9c67-a90e8913ba13" :question/math-academy-id "e-21856"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050571}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425679} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425681}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "2dbaea0b-5765-5b39-b530-0a1d72db0b20" :step/math-academy-id 602021}] :activity/title "Differentiating and Integrating Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221474}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447646} :activity/id #uuid "34e3151a-47c8-57be-a129-fae3a26f1727" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050010 :topic/id #uuid "2602318d-f33f-5fd6-9721-6e9791bc0896" :topic/math-academy-id 6383} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30654a61-bca2-5d45-8dfd-6666c2232131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315391} {:answer/id #uuid "481d3ffb-b826-5c26-abd5-24a0975fb43c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315535} {:answer/id #uuid "57b480dc-e5e7-550b-882b-cb01488fd141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315626} {:answer/id #uuid "5a702db4-464c-52c5-ae25-6568483b6169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315391} :answer-field/id #uuid "118aaf33-e092-57ae-ad75-e10e6a5a7996" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0369724f-0d94-5066-8c6d-06eee2316ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315136} {:answer/id #uuid "2272ec4e-6d60-5d4a-8e26-dfa44c7f7097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315308} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4d0ed2-